!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/34/ltr8/http/deployment-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/34/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  A deployment descriptor: how ONE INSTANCE is configured, as against what a document must be (a schema)
  or what an endpoint offers (an API description).

  [TSON-DATA] §8.2 introduced a security control with no artifact -- a restriction level, a unit and an
  optional script set, enforced by default, with nothing said about where the configuration lives. This is a
  proposal for where. UPSTREAM.md carries the argument; the short form is that the two obvious homes are both
  wrong, and for reasons stronger than the decision being orthogonal:

  A SCHEMA cannot hold it. An artifact that declared its own strictness would choose its own check, and a
  document carrying homographs would simply declare the level that admits them. §3.5 also makes a published
  schema immutable and §2.2.1 lets it be hash-pinned, while a policy must move -- confusables.txt updates,
  threat models change -- so raising a level would mint a new identity and every document pinning the old one
  would keep the old policy for good. [TSON-DATA] §8.3 settles it independently: skeleton distinctness does
  not compose across !!import, so the policy is a property of the merged namespace rather than of any one
  schema.

  An API DESCRIPTION cannot hold it either, being a schema here and inheriting both, and it would put policy
  in a contract -- raising a token policy would mean publishing a new description, which is the friction that
  gets a control switched off.

  THIS DOCUMENT IS DATA, NOT A SCHEMA, and the line is worth stating. An API description must be a schema
  because `request: order` is a type reference the resolver resolves; that is the whole argument for it. A
  descriptor references no types: a level is an enum member, a host is text, and a schema host is an
  identity, which is a URI. Nothing here needs a namespace, so nothing here needs to be a schema.

  Two rules hold, or self-certification returns by the back door:

    1. A processor is HANDED a descriptor; it does not go looking for one. A descriptor is diffable and
       reviewable where an environment variable is not, but a runtime that loads whatever is on its path
       still lets a container image change a security policy with no code diff.
    2. NO DOCUMENT MAY NAME ONE. There is no !!import of a descriptor and no reference to one from a schema
       or a data document. The moment a document can point at a descriptor, it picks its own enforcement.

  Rule 2 is why `acceptance_profile` below is a separate type rather than this one served as-is: what a
  counterparty may see is a PROJECTION, derived from the descriptor, never the descriptor.
  """
{
  @doc:"""
    A hand-written copy of io.ltr8.tson.compiler.TsonUnicodePolicy.Level, which copies UTS #39 §5.2's six
    levels. TsonDeploymentSchemaTest holds the two together.

    The names are UTS #39's own, deliberately. §8.2 makes a point of it -- "the level is a UTS #39 name, so
    two implementations agree on it without reading this document" -- so a descriptor that invented OPEN and
    RESTRICTED would spend an interop property the series bought on purpose.
    """
  restriction_level => !enum [
    ASCII_ONLY SINGLE_SCRIPT HIGHLY_RESTRICTIVE MODERATELY_RESTRICTIVE MINIMALLY_RESTRICTIVE UNRESTRICTED
  ]

  @doc:"""
    What the level is applied to: the whole name, or each `_`/`-` delimited segment of it. §8.2 calls the
    unit "this series' one refinement" on UTS #39, and names it as the relaxation to reach for FIRST --
    per-segment Highly Restrictive still refuses every within-word homograph while admitting the compounds
    that mix a Latin abbreviation with a name in another script.

    Absent means WHOLE. It is a separate field rather than more levels because level and unit are
    independent, and folding them would give twelve names where UTS #39 has six.
    """
  policy_unit => !enum [WHOLE SEGMENT]

  @doc:"""
    A Unicode script, as [UAX #24]'s `Script` property names one: either the long alias (`Latin`,
    `Canadian_Aboriginal`) or the short alias, which is its ISO 15924 code (`Latn`, `Cans`). Both are
    enumerated in the UCD's PropertyValueAliases.txt, and matching is case-insensitive, so `Latin`, `LATIN`,
    `latin` and `Latn` all name one script.

    The pattern constrains SHAPE and not membership, and the distinction is the whole design of this field.
    Membership is a set of 171 values that grows with every Unicode release, and this is a PUBLISHED schema
    (§3.5): an enum of them would need a new schema identity each time the UCD moved, which is exactly the
    trap the policies themselves are here to avoid. A shape, on the other hand, never goes stale -- and it
    is deliberately loose enough to admit every spelling above, including the mixed-case long aliases such
    as `SignWriting` that a tighter rule would refuse.

    No anchors: a `pattern` is I-Regexp (RFC 9485, the dialect the kernel's `regex` atom is pinned to), and
    an I-Regexp match is against the whole token. `^` and `$` are not anchors there -- writing them makes a
    pattern nothing can satisfy.

    So it catches a typo of form and not of content. `Cyrrilic` is well-shaped and is not a script, and it
    fails where the authoritative table is: when the descriptor is read. Spelling is canonicalised there
    too, so two descriptors naming one set produce one policy however they spelled it.
    """
  script_name => !text ^ { pattern: "[A-Za-z][A-Za-z_]*" }

  @doc:"""
    One §8.2 policy. `permitting` names an additional admitted script combination -- the mechanism §5.2
    itself uses for Latin+Japanese and its siblings, and the narrowest relaxation available: a deployment
    that knows it is Russian says [Latin Cyrillic] rather than dropping a level and losing the rule
    everywhere else.
    """
  unicode_policy => {
    level:      restriction_level
    unit:       policy_unit?
    permitting: [script_name]?
  }

  @doc:"Where this instance listens. Absent members leave the caller's own defaults alone."
  listener => {
    host: text?
    port: uint16?
  }

  @doc:"""
    One deployment.

    `identifiers` governs declared names and `tokens` every token off the stream, values included. The two
    default opposite ways round for reasons §8.2 gives -- a name is an interface and a homograph in one is an
    attack, while data may legitimately be a Cyrillic display name -- so a descriptor stating neither is
    asking for exactly that, not for nothing.

    `schema_hosts` is the fetch allow-list: which schema identities this deployment will load over the
    network. Empty means none, which is a real posture rather than a missing setting -- a service that
    resolves only what it was given fetches nothing. It is deliberately NOT in `acceptance_profile`: a
    counterparty has no business knowing which origins this deployment trusts.
    """
  deployment => {
    name:         text
    listener:     listener?
    identifiers:  unicode_policy?
    tokens:       unicode_policy?
    schema_hosts: [text]?
  }

  @doc:"""
    What a counterparty may see: the answer to "what will this endpoint accept?", and nothing else.

    Served at a well-known path rather than at an identity's, and the reason is the same one that makes a
    descriptor unnameable. In this series everything with an identity is served at its identity's path
    (§2.2.1); a descriptor is precisely the artifact that must NOT have an identity, so a well-known path is
    right for it for the same reason it would be wrong for a schema.

    It is DERIVED from a descriptor rather than written beside one. The fetch allow-list is internal
    topology, and two documents maintained by hand drift.

    A PROFILE IS A HINT AND THE REFUSAL IS THE AUTHORITY. This can be cached, and a policy can change under
    it; only the 4xx a request actually receives says what applied to that request. §8.2 is why -- it puts
    the policy in the refusal, "under a stated policy and a stated data version".

    `unicode_data_version` is that second half. §8.3 marks all three rules unstable across Unicode
    releases, so a refusal is only interpretable against the data that produced it, and two conforming
    processors may legitimately disagree about one name. A refusal names it too; this states it once, for
    a client that would rather know before it sends than after it is refused.
    """
  acceptance_profile => {
    name:                 text
    identifiers:          unicode_policy?
    tokens:               unicode_policy?
    unicode_data_version: text?
  }
}
